PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Key facts preserved: n=10,k=5 ⇒ EJR threshold ℓ·2. Two decisive coalitions: Sx={1,2,3,8,9,10} (size 6, common intersection {x1,x2,x3}, ℓ=3) and Sy={4,5,6,7} (size 4, common intersection {y4,y5,y6}, ℓ=2). Thus every valid W must give ≥3 reps to ≥1 member of Sx and ≥2 reps to ≥1 member of Sy.

Sx forces |W∩X|≥2 (with z1) or |W∩X|=3; examples achieving |W∩A1|=2 are {x1,x2,z1,y4,y5} (voters 8-10 each obtain exactly 3) and |W∩A1|=3 are {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5}. Attempts at |W∩A1|=4 (e.g., two X + y1 + z3 + z1) leave Sy with <2 approved members, violating EJR; likewise 0 or 1 X-candidate leaves no member of Sx with 3 reps. Hence attainable interval is exactly [2,3] (choice H). All other answers contain at least one demonstrable error in coalition size, ℓ-value or explicit counter-example.

Round-1 public messages confirm the same interval: 12 colleagues explicitly defend H with the two concrete realizing committees above; the 5 dissenters (A/B) repeatedly overlook the sub-coalition {2,3,8,9,10} (size 5, still ℓ=2) or fail to reserve two seats for Sy. No new evidence alters the range.}

