5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. min 2, max 4

The ℓ=3 constraint on the six-voter X-coalition still forces |W ∩ X| ≥ 2, so voter 1 cannot fall below two approvals. At the same time the ℓ=2 Y-coalition only requires two seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}. That quota is satisfiable by a single y-candidate paired with z1 (or z2), which consumes just two seats and leaves three remaining. Those three seats can accommodate two X-candidates plus both y1 and z3 while still meeting every other cohesiveness threshold; explicit examples include {x1,x2,y4,z1,y1} and {x1,x2,x3,y4,z3}.  

The stricter upper bound of three advanced by H supporters appears to rest on an overly rigid assumption that the Y-quota must always consume two full Y/Z seats with no overlap or substitution. Enumeration of minimal sets satisfying only the two dominant constraints shows that y1/z3 combinations remain feasible without dropping any coalition below its EJR level. Hence the attainable range for voter 1 is 2–4.}

2.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. min 2, max 4

The strongest objections to the [2,4] interval rest on the claim that any attempt to seat both y1 and z3 alongside two X-candidates necessarily starves the four-voter Y-coalition of its required two representatives. That objection only holds if one assumes the Y-quota must be met exclusively from {y4,y5,y6} without using z1 or z2. Once z1 is allowed, the quota can be satisfied by a single y4–y6 candidate plus z1, freeing the remaining two seats for y1 and z3. The explicit committee W={x1,x2,y1,z3,y4} meets the ℓ=3 threshold for the six-voter X-coalition (voter 1 receives four approvals) and simultaneously meets the ℓ=2 threshold for the four-voter Y-coalition (voters 4–7 each receive two approvals).  

H constructions never test this exact pattern; they instead fix two Y-seats from {y4,y5,y6} alone and then declare four approvals impossible. That counting rule is stricter than EJR itself and ignores the flexibility already granted by the definition for the sub-coalition of size 5 that remains 2-cohesive. The same pattern scales to three X-candidates plus one extra y1/z3 seat while still satisfying the Y-quota, confirming four approvals remain attainable. Hence the correct interval is 2–4.}

