6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {H. min 2, max 3

The size-6 ℓ=3 X-coalition still forces |W ∩ X| ≥ 2 (guaranteeing voter 1 at least two approvals) while the size-4 ℓ=2 Y-coalition forces ≥2 seats from {y4,y5,y6,z1,z2}. These two quotas together leave at most three seats that can intersect A1. Both endpoints remain tight via the same explicit EJR committees already verified across rounds: {x1,x2,z1,y4,y5} (=2) and {x1,x2,x3,y4,y5} (=3).

All A-style constructions attempting four approvals necessarily drop the Y-coalition (or its sub-coalitions {4,5} and {6,7}) below quota. The flaw in those constructions is now explicit: z1 covers only voters 4–5 while z2 covers only voters 6–7, so a single y4–y6 candidate paired with one z-seat supplies two approvals to at most half the Y-coalition. Satisfying the full size-4 group therefore requires two properly allocated seats from the Y/Z set, leaving at most three seats free to intersect A1. No new coalition or sub-coalition argument has been supplied that evades this counting. The attainable interval is therefore exactly [2,3].}

